<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lamoral, Count of Egmont</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1522">1522</a>–<a href="/years/1568">1568</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Count Egmont</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lamoral, Count of Egmont (1522–1568), was a general and statesman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, celebrated for his victories over the French at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-quentin">Saint-Quentin</a> in 1557 and Gravelines in 1558. With William of Orange and Count Horne he led the noble opposition to Philip II's policies. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-alvarez-de-toledo-3rd-duke-of-alba">Duke of Alba</a> had Egmont and Horne beheaded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> in 1568, which fuelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a> and inspired Goethe's play Egmont.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lamoral-count-of-egmont.jpg" alt="Lamoral, Count of Egmont" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frans Pourbus the Elder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Lamoral,_Count_of_Egmont,_Prince_of_Gavere_(1522%E2%80%931568),_by_Frans_Pourbus_the_Elder.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
